  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Effective reintegration into education and family life (South Africa)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Both Uitsig, on the outskirts of Cape Town, and Beaufort West have high levels of poverty, unemployment, violence, drug and alcohol abuse and high levels of HIV/AIDS infections. Poverty, violence and their own experiences of abuse and the resulting lack of self-esteem leads to parents to alcohol or drug addiction, crime and/or prostitution and the inability and confidence to care for or protect their children. Faced with neglect and abuse from family members, children opt for a survival strategy that appears to address their need for belonging. This usually involves dropping out of school, gang and street involvement, alcohol and drug abuse or high-risk sexual behaviour, continuing the negative cycle into the next generation. A lack of an understanding of the complex needs of children and their families, insufficient allocation of resources and limited staff capacity leads to poor implementation of these policies in many impoverished communities. This project will help children integrate into mainstream education, and work with teachers and parents to prevent future drop-out by siblings. Community members will lead the interventions, which include building trusted relationships with street-involved children and their families, addressing children&apos;s literacy and numeracy needs and assisting reinsertion into mainstream school through. For families where the needs are most acute, interventions will include emergency shelter and/or intensive, therapeutic support aimed at ensuring long-term change and support for vulnerable children. An action research component will enable project staff, including community staff, document and improve strategies for improving child-parent relationships. Ten local community organisations working with children that live in similar circumstances will be trained, based on project findings so that a wider group of children can benefit from this project.</narrative>
  </description>
